course: history
lesson: 51
title: China: five thousand years and the oldest dynasties
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your friend Mei says China has 5,000 years of history and is the oldest country on Earth; the history teacher says Egypt is older. Who is right? A bit of both, and finding out how is the fun.
explain: China's written history begins with the Shang kings about 3,200 years ago, who scratched questions on turtle shells and ox bones; the «5,000 years» also counts legendary rulers like the Yellow Emperor. Egypt had a state earlier, but China's civilisation has gone on without stopping for an amazingly long time.
explain: Think of a family photo album: the newest pages have clear photos, the oldest have only drawings and grandma's stories. Early Chinese history is like that: the Shang pages have writing, the Xia pages are still debated, and the Yellow Emperor is a beloved story.
explain: Later the Zhou kings said Heaven gave a just ruler the right to rule, and wise teachers like Confucius and Laozi wrote ideas that people still read today.
fact: China has one of the longest continuous civilisations in the world, but the states of Egypt and Mesopotamia began earlier; «five thousand years» is a traditional phrase that includes legendary times.
fact: The Yellow Emperor, Huangdi, is a legendary ruler said to have lived about 4,700 years ago; many Chinese honour him as a cultural ancestor, though historians cannot prove he lived.
fact: Tradition names the Xia as the first dynasty (a family of rulers, one after another), about 2070–1600 BC; some archaeologists link it to the Erlitou site, but no Xia writing has been found, so whether it was real is still debated.
fact: The Shang dynasty, about 1600–1046 BC, carved questions on turtle shells and ox bones, heated them and read the cracks as answers from their ancestors; these oracle bones hold the oldest known Chinese writing.
fact: Oracle bones were noticed around 1899, when people were selling them as «dragon bones» for medicine.
fact: Modern Chinese characters descend from Shang writing, but the script changed a lot, so an ordinary reader today cannot simply read an oracle bone.
fact: The Zhou dynasty, 1046–256 BC, taught the Mandate of Heaven: a ruler governs while he rules justly, and disasters and revolts show he has lost the right; rebels could use the same idea.
fact: Confucius, 551–479 BC, taught respect for family, love of learning, fair rulers and good behaviour; his ideas shaped China, Korea, Japan and Vietnam.
fact: Confucius' golden rule says: what you do not want for yourself, do not do to others.
fact: Laozi is the traditional author of the Dao De Jing, a short book about following the natural «Way» (Dao); his very long life is a legend, and some historians doubt he was one person.
fact: The Art of War, credited to Sun Tzu around the 5th century BC, is a classic about strategy, widely read today in armies and business schools.
fact: In the Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods, 770–221 BC, China was split into rival states fighting each other, until the state of Qin united it in 221 BC.
fact: China made silk for thousands of years and guarded the method; a famous story says monks smuggled silkworm eggs to Byzantium around 552 AD.
exercise: What do historians mean when they call China's civilisation «longest continuous»? | It has gone on without a full break for thousands of years | It was the very first state on Earth, older than Egypt and Sumer | It never had any wars | It was always one country
exercise: Which had a state earlier than China? | Egypt | Australia | Canada | Brazil
exercise: Who was the Yellow Emperor? | A legendary ruler honoured as a cultural ancestor | The first astronaut of China | A real king whose name is proven by many oracle bones | A Mongol khan
exercise: Why is the Xia dynasty still debated? | No Xia writing has been found | Its palace is far too big to dig up | Nobody has ever gone to look for it | It was really in Egypt, not China
exercise: Which site do some archaeologists link to the Xia? | Erlitou | Pompeii in Italy | Stonehenge | Machu Picchu
exercise: What did Shang kings do with oracle bones? | Heated them and read the cracks as answers | Used them as coins at the market to buy rice and silk | Played dice with them | Built houses from them
exercise: How were oracle bones found around 1899? | People were selling them as «dragon bones» for medicine | A farmer's dragon-shaped plough dug them up | They fell out of a crack in the Great Wall after a storm | Marco Polo brought them home
exercise: Can an ordinary reader today read an oracle bone? | No, the script changed a lot, though it is the same writing family | Yes, the writing is exactly the same as in any Chinese newspaper today | Yes, it is written in English | No, it is written in Latin
exercise: What was the Mandate of Heaven? | The idea that Heaven lets a just ruler rule and can take the right away | A law that says the emperor can never die and must rule for ever and ever | A calendar of the stars | A map of the heavens
exercise: How could rebels use the Mandate of Heaven? | By saying disasters showed Heaven had changed its mind | By asking the emperor politely for a written ticket to rebel | By building a taller palace | They could not use it at all
exercise: What is Confucius' golden rule? | What you do not want for yourself, do not do to others | Always try to win, whatever it costs you or anybody else | Never talk to your teachers | Eat rice every morning
exercise: Which countries were shaped by Confucius' ideas? | China, Korea, Japan and Vietnam | Only China, and nowhere else on Earth | Ancient Egypt, Greece and the Roman Empire | Britain, Ireland and the United States
exercise: What does «Dao» mean in Laozi's book? | The Way | The Wall | The Dragon | The Great War
exercise: What is The Art of War about? | Strategy | Landscape painting | Cooking rice and noodles | Silk farming
exercise: What was China like in the Warring States period? | Split into rival states that fought each other | One peaceful country under one kind emperor at last | Ruled by the Romans from far away | Empty of people
exercise: Which state united China in 221 BC? | Qin | Zhou | Ming | Song
exercise: How did silk-making reach Byzantium, by the famous story? | Monks smuggled silkworm eggs there | A dragon carried them | Marco Polo sold them to the emperor in Constantinople | They flew on a kite
test: Mei says China is the oldest country, the teacher says Egypt is older. What is the wisest answer? | Egypt's state was older; China's civilisation is among the longest continuous | Mei is completely right | The teacher is completely right and China is a young country, only a few hundred years old | Both are wrong; Rome is the oldest
test: mei says shes got a 3000 year old bone with writing and she can read it lol. can she? | Not really; she might spot a few characters, the script changed a lot | Yes, Chinese writing has never changed at all, so anyone can read it easily | No, oracle bones have no writing | Yes, because it is in English
test: The teacher says «the Xia didn't exist», Mei says «it did». What should a careful historian say? | It is debated; some link it to Erlitou, but no writing is found | It is proven by Xia books found in a library in the old capital | It is a Japanese legend | It was found in 1974 by farmers
test: Grandpa says the Yellow Emperor is our ancestor, the teacher says he's a myth. Which is fairest? | He is a legend that many honour as a cultural ancestor | He is proven by DNA tests on the bones found in his royal tomb | He was a Roman emperor | He was the first Ming emperor
test: If floods, famine and revolts all came in the same years, what would people under the Zhou think? | The ruler might have lost the Mandate of Heaven | Heaven was very pleased | The ruler would rule for ever | Nothing would change
test: Ugh, Mei won't shut up — «every general in the world learns Sun Tzu». True? | Too much; it is widely read, not by every general | Yes, it is the law in every army | No, nobody reads it any more | It is a cookbook, not about war
test: A cartoon shows Laozi living to 200 and riding an ox to the stars. What is closer to history? | The book is real; his long life is a legend | He really lived 200 years, and the ox is in a museum | He never wrote anything at all | He was a Shang king
test: Mei says China was always one big country. What would the teacher point to? | The Warring States, when rival states fought until 221 BC | The Great Wall, which proves it was one | The Roman Empire | The Moon landing
test: Why did merchants once sell oracle bones in medicine shops? | People thought they were «dragon bones» | They were made of gold | They tasted of honey | They were silkworm cocoons
test: Mei says China kept silk secret for exactly 3,000 years. What is wiser to say? | China guarded it a very long time; the exact length is unknown | Exactly 3,000 years, to the very day, as written in the old books | Silk was never secret | Silk came from Rome
test: What would we need to prove the Xia dynasty for sure? | Writing from Xia times that names it | A film about the Xia | A bigger museum | A vote by schoolchildren
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the antiquity of Chinese civilisation. | It ranks among the longest continuous civilisations, although Egyptian statehood is earlier | It constitutes, beyond dispute, the earliest state upon the Earth, preceding Egypt and Sumer | Its written records commence five thousand years ago | It originated subsequent to the Roman Empire
exam: Identify the material upon which the earliest known Chinese writing was inscribed. | Turtle shells and ox bones employed for divination | Sheets of paper manufactured by Cai Lun | Silk banners of the Ming court | Clay tablets imported from Mesopotamia
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the scholarly position in respect of the Xia dynasty? | Its historicity remains debated, no contemporaneous writing having been recovered | It is conclusively attested by numerous Xia inscriptions upon bronze vessels and stone | It is universally regarded as a modern fabrication | It is identified with the Qin unification of 221 BC
exam: State the principle enunciated by Confucius hereinafter termed the golden rule. | That which one does not desire for oneself ought not to be done to others | Victory is to be pursued irrespective of cost | Instruction is to be confined to the nobility | The ruler is exempt from every obligation
